WEBVTT
TITLE Counting semaphores and the bounded buffer: the counter's arithmetic, the three-counter solution, and the occupancy and blocked-process bounds — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A counting semaphore is one integer, its value, plus a queue of sleeping processes.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
P, or wait, takes one permit if the value is positive; otherwise the caller joins the queue.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
V, or signal, hands one permit to a waiter, or raises the value if nobody is waiting.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Two conventions, one arithmetic: value v0 − P + V, and the queue is the part below zero.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
GATE CSE 1998 Q1.31: value 10, then 6 P and 4 V. Nothing blocks, so the value ends at 8.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Each P takes one permit and each V adds one, so the value only depends on the two counts.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The closed form: final value = initial value − number of P + number of V.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The formula holds only while nothing blocks: it needs initial value + V at least P.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Start at 1. The first P takes the only permit; the second and third P operations block.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
A blocked P is queued, not lost: the value stays at zero and two processes wait.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
One V hands its permit to a waiter, so the value is still zero and one process is left waiting.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Queue length = max(0, P − V − v0). Here 3 − 1 − 1 = 1 sleeper remains and the peak was 2.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
GATE CSE 2016 Set 2 Q49: 20 P and 12 V in some order. Largest initial value with a P still blocked?

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Every P removes one permit and every V adds one, so the total is order-independent.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
The final value is v0 − 20 + 12 = v0 − 8, so a P is still blocked exactly when v0 is below 8.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Largest such v0 is 7. In general it is P − V − 1, here 20 − 12 − 1 = 7.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Three counters, three different questions: free slots, items available, and who may touch the buffer.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
empty starts at N, full at 0, mutex at 1. The producer waits for a slot, then for the lock.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
The invariant: empty + full = N in every reachable state, and the item count equals full.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Each side signals what it has just created for the other: the producer signals full, the consumer empty.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Two producers, two consumers, three slots, two items each: every interleaving is searched.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
2360 reachable states, and in none of them are two processes inside the buffer together.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The item count stays between 0 and 3 in every state, and empty + full = 3 in every state.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
No reachable state is stuck, and at most three of the four processes are ever blocked at once.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
GATE CSE 2018 Q40 numbers the counters against their names: empty starts at 0 and counts items.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
The producer waits on the counter of free slots and signals the counter of items.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Assignment A signals the counter it waited on, so the consumer waits for ever while the buffer overruns.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
B and D let the consumer take items that do not exist: the count reaches −4 and −3. Only C is safe.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
How many items can the buffer hold? At most N, and at most the number produced so far.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
How many processes can be blocked at once? At most n − 1, not n.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Why one can always move: a full buffer wakes a consumer, an empty one wakes a producer.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Measured on six configurations: four processes give at most 3 blocked, two processes give 1.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Take the lock before the counter and the consumer can sleep on full while holding mutex.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The search finds that stuck state at every buffer size it tries: 1, 2, 3 and 5 slots.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Signalling full before releasing mutex is safe, but the woken consumer must block again.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Measured: 52 reachable states with the release first, 30 with the signal first, occupancy 2 against 1.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: which counters exist, and what does each one count? Use the question’s own definitions.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: what does each side wait for first, and what does it signal for the other?

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: for value questions use v0 − P + V, and the queue max(0, P − V − v0); order never matters.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step four: for occupancy use empty + full = N; for liveness look for a sleeper holding the lock.

